{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Strong positive bias: model forecasts are consistently too high vs realized levels (often by ~5–15 EUR), especially during/after the regime shift from ~83 down to low-70s. Apply a downward level correction when recent realized trend is negative.",
    "Trend/turning-point lag: model extrapolates prior strength and fails to capture rapid drawdowns; it predicts stabilization/rebound while the market keeps falling. Increase responsiveness to recent slope (e.g., if last 5–10 days are down, force forecast path to continue down for 1–2 weeks).",
    "Overshoot after local highs: following peaks around 82–84, the model projects higher or flat prices, but outcomes mean-revert sharply lower. Add a mean-reversion penalty proportional to distance from a short-term moving average (e.g., 10–20d MA).",
    "Horizon-dependent error: near-term (days 1–3) can be close, but errors widen quickly beyond ~1 week as forecasts drift upward while reality trends down. Use stronger damping (shrink forecasts toward current spot/MA) for horizons >5–7 days.",
    "Volatility underestimation on downside: realized path shows persistent step-downs; model produces choppy but elevated values. Impose asymmetric adjustment: larger downward correction when recent returns are negative than upward correction when returns are positive.",
    "Regime-change detector: if spot breaks below a recent support/MA (e.g., spot < 20d MA by >1–2%), switch to a 'bear regime' where forecast mean level is anchored closer to recent lows rather than recent highs."
  ]
}
